---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import { personalData } from '../data/personal.js';
---

<Layout title="About — Aaditya Shah" description="About Aaditya Shah, his method, interests, and longer horizon.">
  <Header />
  <main id="main-content">
    <header class="page-hero"><div class="shell"><p class="eyebrow">About</p><h1>Questions start the work. Building tells me what I understand.</h1><p>I am Aaditya Shah, a second-year B.Tech student in Computer Science and Engineering at IIT Gandhinagar, graduating in 2029. I am drawn to difficult questions at the meeting point of mathematics, computation, and engineered systems.</p></div></header>
    <section class="section philosophy-interest"><div class="shell"><p class="eyebrow">Also reading</p><p class="section-intro">Alongside computing and mathematics, I enjoy reading philosophy, especially questions about knowledge, consciousness, ethics, and what it means to understand something.</p></div></section>
    <section class="section"><div class="shell about-grid"><aside class="about-aside"><p class="eyebrow">Current focus</p><p>Mathematics and computation<br />Exploring quantum computing<br />Useful tools and automation<br />Cryptanalysis<br />Interactive web systems<br />Design and collaboration<br />Philosophy and questions about knowledge</p></aside><div class="about-copy"><p class="about-lede">The common thread is not a fixed technology stack. It is curiosity: trying to understand why something works, what structure is hiding underneath, whether the explanation can be generalized, and what building it reveals.</p><p>Alongside computing and mathematics, I enjoy reading philosophy, especially questions about knowledge, consciousness, ethics, and what it means to understand something.</p><p>Quantum computing is another thread I am beginning to explore, especially through its mathematical foundations and its different way of thinking about computation.</p><p>Software, to me, is a tool for making ideas concrete. I like building useful tools, reducing repetitive work, automating small processes, and making systems easier to understand and use.</p><p>Right now, the Enigma investigation is my main research thread. At Blithchron, I serve as a Design and Tech Coordinator and have deployed the <a href="https://blithchron.iitgn.ac.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Blithchron 2027 website</a>. MusicLib remains a personal Android project, but Android is one medium among many rather than the direction that defines me.</p></div></div></section>
    <section class="section philosophy-section"><div class="shell philosophy-grid"><div><p class="eyebrow">Design philosophy</p><h2 class="section-title">Make the work understandable.</h2></div><div class="philosophy-copy"><p>This site should feel like opening the working notebook of a thoughtful researcher rather than visiting a personal brand website.</p><p>It should communicate quiet confidence instead of self-promotion, curiosity instead of expertise, and substance instead of decoration.</p><p><strong>The website should not optimize for making me appear impressive. It should optimize for making me understandable.</strong></p></div></div></section>
    <section class="section method-section"><div class="shell"><header class="section-head"><div><p class="eyebrow">How I work</p><h2 class="section-title">A question becomes clearer by moving through a few forms.</h2><p class="section-intro">Every investigation can inherit this method, even when the order bends or a step sends me back to an earlier one.</p></div></header><ol class="method-list"><li><span class="index-number">01</span><strong>Notice</strong><p>Find the thing that does not yet make sense.</p></li><li><span class="index-number">02</span><strong>Read</strong><p>Locate the history, language, and existing explanations.</p></li><li><span class="index-number">03</span><strong>Model</strong><p>Make the structure explicit enough to reason about.</p></li><li><span class="index-number">04</span><strong>Build</strong><p>Turn an idea into a system, simulation, or artifact.</p></li><li><span class="index-number">05</span><strong>Test</strong><p>Look for evidence, counterexamples, and constraints.</p></li><li><span class="index-number">06</span><strong>Explain</strong><p>Write down what changed and what still resists clarity.</p></li><li><span class="index-number">07</span><strong>Return</strong><p>Come back when a better question or method appears.</p></li></ol></div></section>
    <section class="section"><div class="shell horizon"><blockquote>“A website that can grow with the questions.”</blockquote><div class="horizon-copy"><p>This atlas is intentionally designed for a long horizon. Notes can become research. Investigations can become publications or tools. Questions can be revised without pretending they were settled.</p><p>Ten years from now, the subjects may be deeper and the outputs more formal. The underlying method should still feel recognizable.</p></div></div></section>
    <section class="section"><div class="shell horizon"><blockquote>Working on something difficult?</blockquote><div class="horizon-copy"><p>I am always glad to hear from people thinking carefully about mathematics, computation, research, or complex systems.</p><div class="hero-actions"><a class="button primary" href={`mailto:${personalData.socialLinks.mail}`}>Write to me ↗</a><a class="button secondary" href={personalData.socialLinks.github} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub ↗</a></div></div></div></section>
  </main>
  <Footer />
</Layout>
